.TH TOUCHGEST 2
.SH NAME
touchgest \- a touch screen's fingers as pointer events
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "touchgest.m";
touchgest := load Touchgest Touchgest->PATH;
Gest: import touchgest;

Longms: con 500;

Gest: adt {
    new:   fn(verb: string, slop, step: int): ref Gest;
    frame: fn(g: self ref Gest, pts: list of (int, int), now: int):
               list of string;
    tick:  fn(g: self ref Gest, now: int): list of string;
    due:   fn(g: self ref Gest): int;
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A touch screen has a finger where a mouse has a button, and no other
buttons and no wheel.
.I Touchgest
says what its fingers are as a pointer, for a driver that reads a touch
screen and writes
.B /dev/pointer
(see
.IR pointer (3)),
so that a finger means the same on every touch screen: the Raspberry Pi's
panel
.RB ( os/init/touch.b ,
see
.IR touch (3)),
a USB touchscreen
.RB ( os/init/mouseusb.b ),
and, made the same way by
.IR emu (1)'s
SDL backend, a phone's or a tablet's.
.TP
One finger
is button 1, held while it is down, the pointer following it.
.TP
One held still
for
.B Longms
milliseconds is button 3: button 1 goes up and button 3 down where the
finger is, held until it lifts.
That is every context menu: hold, the menu shows, slide to an item, lift
to choose it.
A finger that moves more than
.I slop
before then is a drag, and one that lifts before then a tap.
.TP
Two dragged
are the wheel: for each
.I step
the fingers move together, button 8 (they moved down) or 16 (up) pressed
and released where they are; moving them up shows what is below, as a
phone scrolls.
Button 1, if the first finger pressed it, goes up first.
The gesture lasts until every finger has lifted, so the last one up does
not press button 1 where it happens to be.
.PP
.B Gest.new
makes one for a screen, whose events are written with
.I verb
.RB ( m
for positions in the screen's pixels,
.B a
for fractions of it, 0 to 65535, for a driver that does not know how big
the screen is);
.I slop
and
.I step
are in the same units.
.B Frame
is given each frame's fingers, the first the pointer, and the time in
milliseconds, and returns the events to write, in order; an empty list of
fingers is none down.
A finger held still sends no new frame, so
.B due
says when a hold falls due (or \-1 when none is waiting to), and
.B tick
is called then, or at any time, and returns button 3's event when it is.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/touchgest.b
.br
.B /module/touchgest.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pointer (3),
.IR touch (3),
.IR wm-osk (1)
